      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Accidental losses or natural mortality almost always cause inequities in family sizes in sib analyses. With unbalanced data, estimates of variance components can still be obtained by the method of moments, but this requires that the definitions of the expected mean squares first be modified appropriately (Table 18.1). All aspects of the unbalanced one-way ANOVA are identical to those outlined for the balanced design, except for the expected among-family mean square, which is no longer $ (\\sigma_{e}^{2} + n\\sigma_{s}^{2}) $, but $ (\\sigma_{e}^{2} + n_{0}\\sigma_{s}^{2}) $, where $ n_{0} $ is a function of the sire-specific family sizes (Table 18.1). Thus, in obtaining estimates of the variance components by the method of moments, we still use Equations 18.12a,b, substituting $ n_{0} $ for n.",
